      pathophysiology: "For metabolic acidosis, Winter's estimate is expected PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3 + 8, with an approximate +/-2 mm Hg range. For metabolic alkalosis, expected PaCO2 is about 40 + 0.7 x (HCO3 - 24), with an approximate +/-2 to 5 mm Hg range depending on the reference and clinical setting; hypoxemia limits compensatory hypoventilation. In acute respiratory acidosis, HCO3 rises about 1 mEq/L and pH falls about 0.08 for each 10-mm Hg PaCO2 rise above 40; in chronic respiratory acidosis, HCO3 rises about 3.5-4 and pH falls about 0.03 per 10. In acute respiratory alkalosis, HCO3 falls about 2 and pH rises about 0.08 for each 10-mm Hg PaCO2 fall below 40; in chronic respiratory alkalosis, HCO3 falls about 4-5 and pH rises about 0.03 per 10. These are approximate population rules anchored near PaCO2 40 and HCO3 24, not exact individual predictions.",
